.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
.TH KGMON 8 "" "KernelGuard" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
kgmon \- KernelGuard alert monitor, response-posture control and escalation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kgmon
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fICOMMAND\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kgmon
reads the alerts of the KernelGuard kernel module (\fB/dev/kernelguard\fR) from a read-only shared-memory ring,
verifies the HMAC-SHA256 of every notification with the per-boot key, and reports them: on standard output, in a CSV
log, to syslog, as desktop notifications, and, if an escalation policy is configured, by running actions.
It needs root (the key ioctl requires \fBCAP_SYS_ADMIN\fR).
.PP
With no command it follows the ring: the backlog first, then new alerts, until interrupted.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B watch
Follow alerts (the default).
.TP
.B status
Show the module's state: mode, state integrity, response posture, the per-CPU mitigation table and the counters.
.TP
\fBposture\fR [\fBset\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
Show the response posture, what raised it, the automatic ceiling, the decay time and the effective scan intervals.
With \fBset\fR, step the posture to \fBnormal\fR, \fBelevated\fR, \fBhigh\fR or \fBfailsafe\fR.
Lowering from HIGH or FAIL-SAFE is refused if the module found its policy state corrupted; unload and reload it.
The change is announced with an authenticated \fBPOSTURE_CHANGED\fR alert.
.TP
.B ack
Acknowledge every alert seen so far. The running monitor stops \fBunacked=\fR escalation for them; the same alert
appearing afterwards is a new incident.
.TP
\fBpolicy check\fR [\fIFILE\fR]
Validate an escalation policy and exit. Errors name the file, the line and the reason.
.TP
.B selftest
Run the SHA-256/HMAC known-answer tests and the escalation-engine unit tests.
.TP
\fBrun\fR [\fB\-\-sensitive\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR...
Execute \fICOMMAND\fR. With \fB\-\-sensitive\fR, first enable, where the kernel supports them, an L1D flush on
context switch, indirect-branch and store-bypass mitigation, and a core-scheduling cookie. Each control reports
whether it is enabled.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-device\fR \fIPATH\fR
Driver node (default \fB/dev/kernelguard\fR).
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-log\fR \fIFILE\fR
Append alerts to \fIFILE\fR as CSV (Time,Level,AlertType,HMAC,Seq,Param1,Param2,Details).
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-syslog\fR
Also send alerts to syslog (facility authpriv).
.TP
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-notify\fR[=\fIUSER\fR]
Desktop notifications through notify-send; as root, \fIUSER\fR selects whose session receives them.
.TP
\fB\-1\fR, \fB\-\-once\fR
Print the backlog and exit. Never runs policy actions.
.TP
.B \-\-new\-only
Skip the backlog.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-max\fR \fIN\fR
Exit after \fIN\fR alerts.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No output on standard output.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-policy\fR \fIFILE\fR
Escalation policy. Default: \fB/etc/kernelguard/policy.conf\fR if it exists.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-policy
Do not load a policy, not even the default file.
.TP
.B \-\-ack\-file \fIFILE\fR
Where \fBkgmon ack\fR leaves its mark (default \fB/run/kernelguard/ack\fR).
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR; \fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the version or the usage.
.SH ESCALATION POLICY
A policy file has two directives:
.PP
.nf
action NAME [timeout=SEC] /absolute/program [ARG...]
rule   NAME [alert=LIST] [level=LEVEL] [throttle=SEC] do=ACTIONS
            [repeat=N/SEC] [unacked=SEC] [then=ACTIONS] [reset=SEC]
.fi
.PP
An \fIincident\fR is one rule, alert type and first alert parameter. \fBdo=\fR actions run when it is first seen and
again after \fBthrottle=\fR seconds (default 60). \fBthen=\fR actions run once when it repeats \fIN\fR times within
\fISEC\fR seconds or is still unacknowledged after \fBunacked=\fR seconds. Levels are \fBinfo\fR, \fBwarning\fR,
\fBcritical\fR and \fBforged\fR. Alert names are those in the README without the \fBALERT_\fR prefix, plus
\fBFORGED\fR, \fBOVERRUN\fR and \fBTAMPER\fR, which \fBkgmon\fR raises itself. A notification whose HMAC fails only
raises \fBFORGED\fR and never matches by content.
.PP
Actions run without a shell, in their own process group, which is killed after the action's timeout (default 10 s).
Their environment holds only \fBPATH\fR and \fBKG_ALERT\fR, \fBKG_ALERT_CODE\fR, \fBKG_TITLE\fR, \fBKG_LEVEL\fR,
\fBKG_LEVEL_NUM\fR, \fBKG_SEQ\fR, \fBKG_TIME\fR, \fBKG_DETAILS\fR, \fBKG_PARAM1\fR, \fBKG_PARAM2\fR, \fBKG_RULE\fR,
\fBKG_ACTION\fR, \fBKG_TIER\fR, \fBKG_COUNT\fR, \fBKG_AGE_S\fR and \fBKG_HOST\fR. The policy file and every action
program must be owned by root and not writable by group or others; a policy that does not load stops the monitor.
.SH SYSTEMD
When started by systemd with \fBType=notify\fR, \fBkgmon\fR announces \fBREADY=1\fR, pings the watchdog at half of
\fBWatchdogSec\fR and announces \fBSTOPPING=1\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/kernelguard
The driver node (mode 0600).
.TP
.B /etc/kernelguard/policy.conf
Escalation policy.
.TP
.B /run/kernelguard/ack
Highest acknowledged sequence number, written by \fBkgmon ack\fR.
.TP
.B /run/kernelguard-monitor.lock
Single-instance lock.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error, 2 on a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
The README shipped with the package (\fB/usr/share/doc/kernelguard/README.md\fR) for the alert protocol, the
module parameters (\fBenforce\fR, \fBauto_enforce\fR, \fBmax_posture\fR, \fBposture_decay_s\fR) and the test suites.
